# (topic, kind, marks, question, answer). kind: theory / calculation / scenario / paper. MCQ entries carry options.
QA = [
 ('w01', 'calculation', 2, r'Pixel row [12, 30, 90, 150, 160]. Compute the central-difference derivative at the 3rd and 4th pixels. Where is the edge?', r'3rd: (150 − 30)/2 = 60. 4th: (160 − 90)/2 = 35. The edge is strongest around the 3rd pixel (largest |Iₓ|).'),
 ('w01', 'theory', 2, r'Why does the lecture use u, v for pixel coordinates, and what order does NumPy indexing use?', r'x, y, z are reserved for 3D coordinates. Images are indexed <code>img[v, u]</code> = [row, column] with the origin at the top-left (W1 p59).'),
 ('w01', 'theory', 2, r'Give two failure modes that the Week 1 lecture lists for real robotic vision systems, with the task they affect.', r'Any two from W1 p33: VO fooled by a moving object; SLAM by two similar-looking places; detection by novel objects mistaken for known ones; grasping by multiple possible grasps or occlusion; learned policies by situations unlike the training data.'),
 ('w02', 'theory', 3, r'Explain why ORB is a better real-time baseline than SIFT, and when you would switch to SIFT.', r'ORB = oriented FAST + rotated BRIEF: binary descriptors (Hamming XOR+popcount), fast on CPU, low memory, in OpenCV. Switch to SIFT for large scale or viewpoint changes, weak texture, or when fewer but more reliable matches are needed, at a higher compute cost (W2 p102).'),
 ('w02', 'calculation', 1, r'Hamming distance between 11001010 and 10011011?', r'XOR = 01010001 → <b>3</b> bits differ.'),
 ('w02', 'theory', 2, r'What two things must a SuperGlue-style matcher decide that a nearest-neighbour matcher does not?', r'Which points correspond (jointly and consistently, one-to-one) <b>and</b> which points have no match at all (dustbin), using context within and across images.'),
 ('w03', 'calculation', 3, r'A camera has f = 4 mm and 2 µm pixels, principal point (640, 360). Where does the camera-frame point (−0.5, 0.25, 5) m project?', r'f = 4e−3/2e−6 = 2000 px. u = 640 + 2000·(−0.5)/5 = <b>440</b>; v = 360 + 2000·0.25/5 = <b>460</b>.'),
 ('w03', 'calculation', 3, r'Stereo rig: f = 600 px, b = 0.1 m. Disparity is 12 px with ±0.5 px uncertainty. Depth and approximate depth error?', r'Z = 600·0.1/12 = <b>5 m</b>. δZ ≈ Z²δd/(fb) = 25·0.5/60 = <b>0.208 m</b>.'),
 ('w03', 'theory', 2, r'Global vs rolling shutter: which suits a fast drone and why?', r'Global shutter: all pixels are exposed at once, so moving scenes aren’t skewed. Rolling shutter reads rows at different times, which distorts fast motion and violates the pinhole two-view model.'),
 ('w04', 'calculation', 2, r'How many RANSAC iterations does the 5-point algorithm need for w = 0.6, p = 0.99?', r'0.6⁵ = 0.0778; k = log(0.01)/log(0.9222) = 56.9 → <b>57</b>.'),
 ('w04', 'theory', 3, r'List three degenerate or near-degenerate situations for estimating F and why each is a problem.', r'Pure rotation (no baseline, so no epipolar geometry); planar scene (a homography explains the data, F not unique); small baseline / distant points (weak parallax, noise dominates); also forward motion near the epipole, dynamic scenes, rolling shutter (W4 p35).'),
 ('w04', 'theory', 2, r'Why can’t two monocular images give the metric length of t?', r'Scaling the scene and t by the same factor s gives identical images: K(R·sX + s·t) = s·K(RX + t), and the projection divides by depth. So only the direction is observable.'),
 ('w05', 'calculation', 2, r'Points: predicted (412, 233), observed (409, 229). Threshold 4 px. Inlier?', r'Residual (−3, −4), norm <b>5 px</b> &gt; 4 → <b>outlier</b>.'),
 ('w05', 'theory', 3, r'Describe the Arun et al. 3D–3D alignment in four steps.', r'(1) centroids of both sets; (2) subtract them; (3) H = Σ x̄ₜ₋₁ x̄ₜᵀ, SVD H = USVᵀ, R = VUᵀ (check det = +1); (4) t = x̄ₜ − R x̄ₜ₋₁. The camera motion is the inverse.'),
 ('w05', 'scenario', 3, r'A monocular AR headset starts with no map. Outline how PTAM initialises and why its arbitrary scale is acceptable.', r'Track ~1000 points while the user moves; 5-point E + RANSAC; triangulate; BA; assume 10 cm of motion for scale; align the dominant plane (RANSAC) with z = 0. AR only needs a consistent scale to anchor virtual content; the absolute metres don’t matter.'),
 ('w05', 'theory', 2, r'When should a new keyframe be added?', r'When the camera has moved or rotated enough (mono rule: baseline / average depth &gt; 10–20%), when too many tracked features are lost, or every N frames. Too few hurts the map; too many wastes compute.'),
 ('w06', 'theory', 3, r'Derive in three lines why MAP estimation with Gaussian factors becomes weighted least squares.', r'X* = argmax Π exp(−½ eᵀΣ⁻¹e) → take the log (monotonic): argmax Σ −½ eᵀΣ⁻¹e → negate: <b>argmin Σ eᵀΣ⁻¹e</b> (sum of squared Mahalanobis residuals).'),
 ('w06', 'calculation', 2, r'Prior N(10, 2²) and measurement N(12, 1²). Fused mean and σ?', r'Weights 1/4 and 1: mean = (10·0.25 + 12·1)/1.25 = <b>11.6</b>; var = 1/1.25 = 0.8 → σ ≈ <b>0.894</b>.'),
 ('w06', 'theory', 2, r'Is the graph with edges x0–x1 (variable–variable) and a factor attached to one variable a factor graph?', r'No: factor graphs are bipartite, and every edge must connect a variable node to a factor node. Direct variable–variable edges are not allowed.'),
 ('w07', 'theory', 3, r'Compare ATE and RPE: what each measures, and one weakness of ATE.', r'ATE: RMSE of absolute positions after a similarity alignment, i.e. global accuracy. RPE: error of relative motions over a step, i.e. local accuracy and drift rate. ATE weakness: one early rotation error inflates all later errors; it needs alignment; one number hides when errors occurred.'),
 ('w07', 'calculation', 1, r'Accelerometer bias 0.02 m/s². Position error after 30 s of pure integration?', r'½·0.02·30² = <b>9 m</b>.'),
 ('w07', 'scenario', 3, r'A delivery robot loses tracking in a dark loading bay, then sees the main hall again. In ORB-SLAM3 terms, what happens?', r'Tracking is lost → relocalisation is tried in all Atlas maps; if it fails, a new active map starts. When the hall (a different, non-active map) is recognised by place recognition, <b>map merging</b> fuses the maps; revisiting a place in the same map would trigger <b>loop closure</b> (pose graph + full BA).'),
 ('w08', 'theory', 2, r'Your linear probe on frozen features already reaches the target accuracy. What does this tell you?', r'The pretrained representation already contains the information needed. Full fine-tuning isn’t required (and risks overfitting with little data).'),
 ('w08', 'calculation', 2, r'ViT on a 384×384 image with 16×16 patches: how many tokens? With 32×32 patches?', r'(384/16)² = <b>576</b> (+1 class token). (384/32)² = <b>144</b>, which means (576/144)² = 16× fewer attention pairs.'),
 ('w08', 'theory', 2, r'Equivariance vs invariance, with a CNN example.', r'Equivariant: shifting the input shifts the feature map (convolution). Invariant: the output is unchanged by the shift (needs pooling, global aggregation or training). CNNs are equivariant by design, not automatically invariant.'),
 ('w09', 'theory', 2, r'Occupancy network vs DeepSDF: what does each predict for a query point?', r'Occupancy: the probability that the point is inside or occupied (surface at p = 0.5). DeepSDF: the signed distance to the nearest surface (&lt;0 inside, 0 on, &gt;0 outside), which is richer because it gives distance.'),
 ('w09', 'theory', 2, r'Why does Depth Anything train its teacher on synthetic data?', r'Synthetic depth is clean, dense and has fine detail, unlike noisy real depth labels. The teacher then transfers that knowledge to real images through pseudo-labels on 62M+ images.'),
 ('w09', 'scenario', 3, r'Your planner needs to know which space is free for a robot arm. Which representation do you choose and why not a NeRF?', r'A voxel/occupancy grid or an SDF: it answers free/occupied queries directly and gives distances for collision checking. A NeRF stores density implicitly, is expensive to query, and good rendering doesn’t validate occupancy (transparent or reflective surfaces).'),
 ('w10', 'calculation', 3, r'GT boxes: 4. Detections by confidence: TP, FP, TP, TP, FP. Precision and recall after each detection? AP (all-point)?', r'P: 1, 0.5, 0.667, 0.75, 0.6. R: 0.25, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 0.75. Monotone precision at recall steps: 1 (to 0.25), 0.75 (to 0.5), 0.75 (to 0.75) → AP = 0.25·1 + 0.25·0.75 + 0.25·0.75 = <b>0.625</b>.'),
 ('w10', 'theory', 2, r'Explain non-maximum suppression and why DETR doesn’t need it.', r'NMS keeps the highest-scoring box and removes same-class boxes overlapping it above an IoU threshold, deleting duplicates. DETR’s one-to-one Hungarian matching during training penalises duplicate predictions, so the queries learn to predict distinct objects.'),
 ('w10', 'theory', 2, r'Closed set, open set, open vocabulary: define each in one line.', r'Closed: choose among known classes. Open set: detect known classes and flag unknowns as “unknown”. Open vocabulary: detect objects named by arbitrary text prompts (CLIP-style image–text alignment).'),
 ('w11', 'theory', 2, r'Sigmoid vs softmax for segmentation outputs: when do you use each?', r'Softmax over C logits for mutually exclusive classes per pixel; an element-wise sigmoid when a pixel can carry several labels (non-exclusive).'),
 ('w11', 'theory', 3, r'Give the parameters of a planar and a 6-DoF parallel-jaw grasp.', r'Planar: (x, y, θ, w, q). 6-DoF: (x, y, z, φ, θ, ψ, w, q). Here w is the opening width and q the quality/confidence.'),
 ('w11', 'paper', 3, r'Contact-GraspNet: success 90.20% vs 80.39% for the segmentation + CollisionNet pipeline. By what fraction is the failure rate cut?', r'Failures 9.80% vs 19.61% → about <b>50%</b> lower (“cutting the failure rate in half”). Attempts fell from 67 to 59.'),
 ('w12', 'calculation', 2, r'VPR: 80 matches returned, 60 correct; 120 queries have a true match. Precision, recall, F1?', r'P = 60/80 = 0.75; R = 60/120 = 0.5; F1 = 2·0.375/1.25 = <b>0.6</b>.'),
 ('w12', 'theory', 2, r'Why is hard-negative mining important when training VPR descriptors?', r'Easy negatives are already far apart and give no gradient. Lookalike places (perceptual aliasing) are the informative ones; pushing them apart teaches discrimination.'),
 ('w12', 'scenario', 3, r'Night-time loop closures in a repetitive car park keep producing false matches. Suggest two fixes from the lecture.', r'Add geometric verification (RANSAC on local features) before accepting a candidate; use sequence information (SeqSLAM-style temporal coherence); raise the threshold to favour precision; use condition-robust features (foundation models / AnyLoc).'),
 ('w13', 'calculation', 3, r'Bins: (conf 0.9, acc 0.75, n = 50), (conf 0.6, acc 0.62, n = 50). ECE? Which bin is over-confident?', r'ECE = 0.5·0.15 + 0.5·0.02 = <b>0.085</b>. The first bin is over-confident (accuracy below confidence); the second is slightly under-confident.'),
 ('w13', 'theory', 2, r'Name the four shift types and give a robotics example of each.', r'Covariate (rain on camera), label/prior (more pallets than people this week), concept (the same appearance now means “keep out”), novel class (a new robot type).'),
 ('w13', 'theory', 2, r'Why can two inputs have the same softmax confidence but different energy scores?', r'Softmax is invariant to adding a constant to all logits (only relative values matter). Energy −log Σ exp z depends on their absolute size: (2, 1, 0) gives −2.41 and (6, 5, 4) gives −6.41.'),
 ('w13', 'scenario', 3, r'An outdoor grasping robot sees high epistemic uncertainty on a new object. What should it do?', r'Treat it as unfamiliar: don’t grasp blindly. Take another viewpoint, slow down, ask a human or use a conservative fallback; log the data for retraining. Uncertainty is only useful if it changes behaviour.'),
 ('w04', 'paper', 3, r'VO Part I says relative scale needs features tracked over at least three frames. Why?', r'The scale ratio compares distances between the same 3D points triangulated from pair (k−2, k−1) and pair (k−1, k): r = ‖X_{k−1,i} − X_{k−1,j}‖/‖X_{k,i} − X_{k,j}‖, which needs the same features in three views.'),
 ('w08', 'paper', 2, r'ViT pre-trained only on ImageNet: ViT-L is worse than ViT-B. With JFT-300M, ViT-L is better. Explain with “inductive bias”.', r'ViT has weak image-specific biases (no built-in locality or translation equivariance), so large models overfit small data. With huge data, scale wins: the patterns are learned instead of built in.'),
]
def render(mcq, openq):
    out = ['''<section data-panel="qa" hidden aria-label="Practice questions"><div><p class="note mono">''' + str(len(QA)) + ''' questions · all 13 weeks</p><h2>Practice Q&A</h2>
<p class="lead">Mixed theory, calculation, scenario and paper-reading questions. Answer on paper first, then reveal and self-mark; ✗ marks feed the weak-topic table on the Study plan. Every number is checked in <code>check_all.py</code> / <code>check_qa.py</code>.</p></div><div class="sub">''']
    for i, (t, kind, m, q, a) in enumerate(QA):
        out.append(openq(f'{t}-qa{i+1}', q, f'<p>{a}</p>', (t.upper().replace('W0', 'W'), kind, f'{m} marks'), num=f'{i+1}'))
    out.append('</div></section>')
    return '\n'.join(out)
